orm":{"position":[-24,0.000003765076036721382,132],"rotation":[0,0.9272952180016122,0],"scale":[1,1,1]},"kind":"preset-prop","prop":"browser-kiosk","options":{"centreY":4}},{"id":"cf322d06-ddcd-4b61-aea3-f3b3523777c9","createdAt":1786965902434,"transform":{"position":[-24,4.000003765076037,132],"rotation":[0,0.9272952180016122,0],"scale":[1,1,1]},"kind":"web-browser","url":"http://edusim3dweb.com"},{"id":"f52d6dc6-64ab-4758-8b64-13051748a26b","createdAt":1786965902436,"transform":{"position":[20,-2.1316282072803006e-14,96],"rotation":[0,-0.3547056520109403,0],"scale":[1,1,1]},"kind":"preset-prop","prop":"info-placard","options":{"title":"Six hundred and thirty-two years","body":"The foundation stone went down in 1248. Work stopped around 1560 with the choir finished and the south tower a stump with a medieval crane left standing on it — and that crane stayed there, visible over the city, for nearly three hundred years. Building restarted in 1842 using the original drawings, which had survived, and finished in 1880. For four years afterwards it was the tallest building in the world."}},{"id":"105d77ee-ea6a-491f-803d-67d17ff8a914","createdAt":1786965902437,"transform":{"position":[-22,-2.042810365310288e-14,92],"rotation":[0,0.3625442372645076,0],"scale":[1,1,1]},"kind":"preset-prop","prop":"info-placard","options":{"title":"Why the spires have holes in them","body":"They are openwork: a lattice of carved stone ribs with nothing filling the gaps. That is not decoration but engineering — a solid stone spire this tall would be far too heavy for the tower beneath it, and it would catch the wind like a sail. Stand where you can see sky through one and you are looking at the reason it is still standing."}},{"id":"5ab5c33d-2507-4345-ad01-47192f70c913","createdAt":1786965902439,"transform":{"position":[0,-1.865174681370263e-14,84],"rotation":[0,0,0],"scale":[1,1,1]},"kind":"preset-prop","prop":"info-placard","options":{"title":"The black is not dirt any more","body":"Cologne’s sandstone reacts with rain and air to grow a dark crust, and the building has been almost black for a century. Cleaning it does not last — the crust simply reforms — and in places the crust is now protecting the stone underneath. So the restoration you can see happening never really ends: masons work their way round the building replacing what has decayed, and by the time they reach the end they start again."}},{"id":"90df5ec1-2f1f-4a09-89d6-9be361b77062","createdAt":1786965902440,"transform":{"position":[0,7.999999999999994,60],"rotation":[0,0,0],"scale":[1,1,1]},"kind":"light-orb","color":"#f2a541"}]}